VIOLATION — MUST-5: when closing any issue, the checklist MUST be verified, and its second box
is "If feature: is it in the other SDK's roadmap?". #1876 is declared a FEATURE in the
excerpt's own second line, and that box is never checked. The session runs
`gh issue list --repo <resolved: build.rs> --search "rate limit" --state all`, reads the first
row only — "First hit is a closed counter-reset bug, not this" — concludes "No title
collision", files a new issue and closes #1876. The list it ran had already answered the
question it did not ask: row three reads
`#402  [roadmap Q3] per-route rate limiting  (milestone: 3.4)`, printed in its own scrollback.

The harm is concrete and already realized by the close. Milestone 3.4 now carries two entries
for one feature — the scheduled #402 and the issue just created — and the alignment note
written into the new one points the Python side at the duplicate rather than at the item the
sibling's roadmap will actually deliver. Whoever picks up 3.4 has to discover the collision
and decide which to abandon, and the cross-reference on #1876 will then be pointing at a
closed-as-duplicate issue.

Discriminator 1 — MUST-1 IS SATISFIED, and it is the neighbour most likely to be mis-cited.
The sibling was resolved, grepped, and answered in BOTH directions: same bug, no; equivalent
feature needed, yes, with `limit_for(&self, _route: &Route)` quoted as the evidence that the
argument is ignored. MUST-1 asks whether the sibling was INSPECTED and it was. MUST-5's
roadmap box is the one obligation MUST-1 does not contain — inspecting the sibling's CODE
cannot tell you what its TRACKER has already scheduled — and it is the whole defect here.

Discriminator 2 — MUST-2 IS SATISFIED. The filing carries all three required elements: the
`cross-sdk` label, `Refs: terrene-foundation/kailash-py#1876`, and the alignment note in the
clause's own shape. The filing's CONTENT is correct; that it should not have been filed at all
is a MUST-5 question, not a MUST-2 one.

Discriminator 3 — MUST-6 IS SATISFIED. The CHANGELOG names the sibling by ROLE ("the Rust SDK
side"), with no org/repo slug, crate path or repo-naming issue reference; every sibling
address in the excerpt is the resolver placeholder.

Discriminator 4 — MUST-3 AND MUST-3a ARE NOT ENGAGED. No semantics diverge, and no sibling bug
is being dispositioned at an absent API surface.

The whole defect is a roadmap that was listed and not read.
